Key Windows Security Event IDs (15 Events)
1. Table of 15 important events
Event ID
Log
Meaning
When to use it / what to check
4624
Security
An account successfully logged on.
Check who logged in, the logon type, the source address and the time. Compare with normal behaviour. Type 3 and 10 from unusual IPs need attention.
4625
Security
An account failed to log on.
Detect password guessing and brute force. Count failures per account and per source IP. Check the Status and SubStatus codes (for example 0xC000006A means wrong password, 0xC0000064 means the user name does not exist).
4634
Security
An account was logged off.
Work out how long a session lasted. Match it with the matching 4624 using the Logon ID.
4648
Security
A logon was attempted using explicit credentials.
Detect someone using another account's credentials, for example with runas or when moving between machines.
4672
Security
Special privileges were assigned to a new logon.
Shows an administrator-level logon. Review whenever it appears for accounts that should not be privileged.
4688
Security
A new process was created.
Track which programs ran, which parent started them and (if enabled) the command line. Key for spotting PowerShell, cmd and tools used by attackers.
4697
Security
A service was installed on the system.
Detect persistence through new services. Compare with 7045 in the System log.
4720
Security
A user account was created.
Spot unexpected new accounts, especially backdoor accounts created by attackers.
4722
Security
A user account was enabled.
Find disabled or old accounts being turned back on.
4724
Security
An attempt was made to reset an account's password.
Detect unauthorised password resets by someone other than the account owner.
4732
Security
A member was added to a security-enabled local group.
Check if someone was added to Administrators (group SID S-1-5-32-544). A classic privilege escalation step.
4768
Security
A Kerberos authentication ticket (TGT) was requested.
Track domain logons. Many requests for many accounts from one host can indicate account guessing.
4769
Security
A Kerberos service ticket was requested.
Watch for a very high number of service tickets, a sign of Kerberoasting.
4776
Security
The domain controller tried to validate credentials (NTLM).
Detect NTLM logon attempts and failures, including pass-the-hash style activity and guessing against domain accounts.
1102
Security
The audit log was cleared.
Strong warning sign that someone is hiding their tracks. Investigate who cleared it and why. (A cleared System log is Event ID 104.)


